  "post": {
    "caption": "Eggs can quickly blacken silver. Tarnish is silver sulfide, made when silver meets sulfur, and sulfur gas in the air is the main cause of tarnish. Eggs and wool both give off sulfur. Thin layers look yellow or blue before turning black, and a thicker layer actually slows further tarnishing. Sterling tarnishes faster because its copper reacts too. Foil in hot baking soda water forms a tiny battery, and the sulfur moves from the silver to the aluminum. Museums wrap silver in cloth that traps sulfur. Silver in regular use stays bright longer.",
